winter sports reigned supreme on the  Zugspitze a' germany's highest mountain  close to the Austrian border at  altitudes over 2000 meters the 20  kilometres of slopes have natural snow  for six months of the year offering  enthusiasts splendid conditions for  downhill racing but if you want to  really stand out from the crowd you can  build your own snowboard or skis like  these participants in a two-day workshop  in fire hunt near the Bavarian town of  garmisch-partenkirchen learn how to make  the models that suit them best off the  rack so to speak you can't always buy  what you need I'm relatively small but I  can't use kids boards because they have  smaller feet now I can adjust the width  and size and that's very good I have  absolutely no idea how skis are made  it's really exciting to learn how much  technology there is in skis the self  built ski starts with a base around  which a metal border is formed then  comes the wood core it's bolstered with  two wood strips to give the boards the  necessary tension workshop instructors  Mishael Aiden shank and axle for Kalla  mainly use ash wood a long fiber wood  that is tough but elastic  mikono hoods shouldn't Foreman you can  shape the wood well and it gives you a  really good resilience it's very  malleable so you get good tension in the  ski when isn't that enables us to adjust  the ski was great my decision to see  what the customer wants I understand  what a cool new is getting hit there  between the layers come sheets of glass  fiber fabric the students learn how to  attach them with a liquid adhesive then  the length width thickness and tension  of the ski are adjusted to their  individual needs  and finally it's time for the personal  design whether a glass of the skiers  favorite brew or photos of friends then  the skis and boards are vacuum packed  and placed in an oven for curing at 80  degrees Celsius this is enough for  Wednesday this is one of the advantages  that we have compared to store-bought  skins we can really adjust hours to  every person who comes to us and  couldn't hide uh packing skills where  they want to ski  we have enough parameters so that we can  bury them in order to individualize the  skis after they've hardened in the oven  for 14 hours the skis and snowboards are  almost ready they just have to be cut  out of the form using a jigsaw then  edges are sanded down to remove any  excess material and unevenness now all  that's left to do is mount the bindings  I'm really pleased with the result my  patterns aren't perfectly centered but  apart from that it's a great ski I had  problems curving the edges because I  couldn't get the arch right at first and  I had trouble cutting it out when it  came out of the oven it was the first  time I'd ever held a jigsaw but I would  they did an okay job except Arella self  built skis are ready for a run on the  slopes he tests his products regularly  up to now he's built 2,000 skis and can  look back on a decade of experience  let's go krinkle there is no cookbook  recipe for making a freeride ski or a  slalom ski it requires lots of  experience lots of days on the slopes  with our experience in building and  testing skis we've compiled a little  one-to-one ski construction method with  a bespoke pair of skis or snowboards  under your feet there's nothing to keep  you from heading downhill this winter
 

